1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:01,740
Where are we going with AI?

2
00:00:03,598 --> 00:00:11,620
So I think we'll learn today, we're not exactly sure, but we are going somewhere and we're going somewhere fast.

3
00:00:12,398 --> 00:00:26,000
So I've been involved with AI since about 1994 when I used a two-layer neural network as part of my supernova classification program in 1994.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,220
before.

5
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:29,796
So, we are now at something that is completely different.

6
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:41,720
I was just in Germany with the Fields Medalist and Abel Prize winners, mathematicians, and a bunch of computer scientists at something called the Heidelberg Laureate Forum.

7
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:49,680
Now, what the mathematicians are experiencing right now, they were not expecting four months ago.

8
00:00:50,190 --> 00:01:02,260
Starting in May, a huge number of unsolved problems in mathematics have suddenly had solutions, emerged through AI-assisted programming.

9
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:10,320
And that has knocked that group, well, as we would say in Australia, for six.

10
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:17,120
But it's really, really come in just like an explosion to their field.

11
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:19,880
And so they're trying to figure out what this means for them.

12
00:01:19,918 --> 00:01:30,960
But ultimately, mathematicians, I think, are generally regarded as being the absolute smartest human beings on the planet, the ones who do pure mathematics.

13
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:37,100
And we have reserved their minds to do these really, really hard math problems.

14
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:40,004
And we've done that for thousands of years.

15
00:01:41,006 --> 00:01:41,220
Okay?

16
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:46,920
And any physicist will tell you the mathematicians are up on the hierarchy of intelligence compared to us.

17
00:01:47,182 --> 00:01:48,000
We agree.

18
00:01:48,526 --> 00:01:49,000
Okay?

19
00:01:49,294 --> 00:02:00,880
And suddenly they have artificial intelligence come in and it can do at least some of the problems that was exclusively their domain.

20
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:02,840
So it's coming in hard.

21
00:02:03,534 --> 00:02:05,000
So what does this mean for society?

22
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,000
Well, unclear.

23
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:11,880
It is going to be transformative, but also very disruptive.

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,240
When suddenly you're used to doing things, you're going to have to change, we're going to have to pivot.

25
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:19,080
And I'm confident mathematicians still have something to do in the future.

26
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:22,436
They can do astronomy, if nothing else, because we have a lot of work for them.

27
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:33,572
But in the end, we have to go through, in my opinion, and remind ourselves that artificial intelligence is a human construct there to serve us.

28
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:47,428
And so I will repeat this in many different forms today, that whatever happens, human accountability is at the forefront of what we need to be thinking.

29
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:49,540
AI is not accountable.

30
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:58,880
Humans are accountable for how we use it, and we need to make sure we build that into what happens, because right now, as near as I can tell, we are not.

31
00:03:00,206 --> 00:03:06,212
When you go in and do something bad with AI, a human needs to be responsible, and we need to build that in.

32
00:03:06,574 --> 00:03:07,980
Brian, thank you very much indeed.

33
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:16,500
So Su-In, you work on explainable AI and often talk of the need to understand what's going on under the hood of AI.

34
00:03:17,070 --> 00:03:18,180
Tell us more.

35
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:28,324
Yeah, so I think my view is that AI is still an evolving technology and its future is predetermined.

36
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:29,580
It's far from it.

37
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:41,360
And then we are actively shaping the future of it And through research directions, we choose to pursue, well, especially people like myself, AI researchers.

38
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:56,900
And this is why I believe that developing new technologies that can improve the interpretability of AI and also accountability and the trustworthiness of AI is of paramount importance.

39
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:16,060
And we need methods, new methods, that can help us understand the complex AI models, the complex inner workings of AI models, and then also help us identify and also address the failure modes of these complex AI models.

40
00:04:16,781 --> 00:04:28,120
So the bottom line is that, you know, this is the technology that is still advancing, and then it depends on us, you know, how we will move forward.

41
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:38,788
And then it would be really important to put emphasis on, you know, this kind of technology, you know, like the technology I'm focusing on: explainably.

42
00:04:39,534 --> 00:04:45,440
So would you say that we are currently too passive in answering that question of where are we going with AI?

43
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:57,740
No, I mean, not necessarily, I'm just saying that, you know, We just shouldn't consider this current AI as just a static thing, right?

44
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:08,080
It's dynamic, it keeps changing, and it's really important for humans to make it better by making more understandable and interpretable.

45
00:05:08,558 --> 00:05:09,640
Thank you very much indeed.

46
00:05:10,446 --> 00:05:11,680
Sangwook, you're a philosopher.

47
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:19,260
You commented that now that with the advent of AI, scientists are rather keener than they were before on having philosophers involved in their discussions.

48
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:21,140
Yeah, relatively speaking, that's right.

49
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,040
So, here you are.

50
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,220
How would you...

51
00:05:26,222 --> 00:05:28,196
After all, I mean, there are...

52
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:38,420
I would like to just agree with the other panellists that we have great uncertainties about AI development and deployment and we have a lot to do.

53
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:44,320
Having said that, I think there is two different dimensions of AI in scientific research.

54
00:05:44,462 --> 00:05:52,320
First one is rather non-controversial issues, which is AI for scientific research.

55
00:05:52,600 --> 00:06:03,140
So, many people agree with you that, you know, using AI actually productivity of a scientific research, I mean, making scientific knowledge is excellent.

56
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:06,440
I mean, its improvement is really amazing.

57
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:20,804
But the second thing is, relatively controversial thing is, because AI is rapidly developing, there are more and more concerns about what would be the kind of final goal of scientific research.

58
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:28,540
and what would be the kind of authenticity of scientists who are doing this kind of making scientific knowledge.

59
00:06:28,558 --> 00:06:38,680
And so, there are lots of discussions going on, whether the kind of activities we call scientific research can continue as it were.

60
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:54,180
I mean, because given that there are some areas of scientific research where AI actually can serve the problems better, but that doesn't necessarily mean that we have to include AI into our scientific research community.

61
00:06:54,240 --> 00:07:02,320
because as you said, the AI doesn't have any kind of value judgment, and they are not accountable at the moment.

62
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:16,740
So there are lots of questions we have confronted which we have to work with in a more comprehensive way, perhaps with philosophers into this research discussion community.

63
00:07:17,166 --> 00:07:21,508
Do you think there's enough discussion of what the goal of scientific research is?

64
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:22,780
Well,

65
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:27,220
maybe it's my just impression, but I don't see that much.

66
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:36,680
I mean, of course, I have to say that even among the scientific communities, there are quite different opinions expressed by scientists.

67
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:42,640
Some scientists are just fascinated by the new possibility of AI deployment in the scientific research.

68
00:07:42,990 --> 00:07:58,116
But some scientists say that, you know, there is nothing, almost nothing human scientists can actually contribute to the production of scientific knowledge so we have to diverge from the conventional understanding of scientific research quite dramatically.

69
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:15,300
So there are lots of difference in opinions but I don't quite see that kind of discussion actually focused on the re-examination of the nature of scientific research and the authenticity of scientists themselves.

70
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:20,220
Oh, I was going to add that.

71
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:28,520
you know, the technologies, the kind of technologies I just mentioned, that improve the transparency and interpretability of complex AI models.

72
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:39,140
I think, you know, that can contribute to, you know, improving the impact of the AI in science.

73
00:08:39,789 --> 00:08:49,732
Because that way, the concepts AI learned would be more aligned with humans, and then that way scientists will better understand what's going on inside the AI models.

74
00:08:49,838 --> 00:09:01,680
and then when there is a wrong conclusion, such as you know, the hypothesis that's not going to work, then, you know, human scientists will easily know that that's the failure mode of AI.

75
00:09:03,694 --> 00:09:04,740
One, yes please Brian.

76
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:15,000
Yeah I and I'm gonna double down on what I said earlier is is that AI and science, I mean, science is very much, I think, the pursuit of knowledge.

77
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:19,580
And then I'm going to add something that is maybe always there but not said.

78
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:25,028
Pursuit of knowledge in service of humanity, broadly defined.

79
00:09:25,240 --> 00:09:33,284
And we have to add in service of humanity now, because I'm worried that we can do things that are against humanity, and we need to be careful.

80
00:09:34,062 --> 00:09:51,080
But ultimately, it's a tool that we use to generate knowledge and service of humanity, but if I come back to my accountability, when I make a discovery, and only AI can understand it, and therefore no one is accountable for it, we have a problem.

81
00:09:51,440 --> 00:10:00,620
And that piece of knowledge is problematic from a very foundational way of having humans in control.

82
00:10:01,230 --> 00:10:04,840
This is exactly what I'm quite concerned.

83
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:24,068
There is a logical possibility, and some of this possibility has been realized in the biomedical research, that the solution or the result AI produced actually works, but humans cannot actually quite understand the causal mechanism behind why it is working.

84
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:41,620
And in this case, if the situation is kind of an emergent situation, like there is no medical care or drugs for a particular disease, but the only viable option is the drug produced by AI, then maybe we have to use it for some ethical reason.

85
00:10:41,740 --> 00:11:05,600
But as long as we cannot actually fully understand the mechanism and the moral reason behind AI solution, even when these solutions are quite successful in dealing with certain problems, we never know whether this success may into catastrophic failure in the future, because that that's the kind of things you mentioned.

86
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:05,900
yeah.

87
00:11:06,126 --> 00:11:07,684
and that's why her work is so important.

88
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:09,160
Thank you.

89
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,100
Thank you very much.

90
00:11:12,430 --> 00:11:28,640
So one thing that I know the audience are in very concerned about is the change in skills that we will be required as all of this comes through, and changing your skill set has always been part of life and part of science, but now it's suddenly accelerated.

91
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:33,440
and you alluded to that at the beginning, with these mathematicians looking for new areas to work, in, perhaps.

92
00:11:34,094 --> 00:11:38,380
So do you want to talk a bit about this concept of de-skilling and the change?

93
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:39,716
Su-In, perhaps?

94
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:41,420
Oh, sorry, I didn't catch your question.

95
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:43,360
No, I didn't see that.

96
00:11:44,430 --> 00:11:47,500
But the change in skills that is going to be required.

97
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:51,200
Change of the skills for what humans should focus on.

98
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:52,120
A human scientist.

99
00:11:52,366 --> 00:11:53,580
Human scientist, yeah.

100
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:56,300
So it depends on the science.

101
00:11:56,480 --> 00:12:05,560
I mean, AI has the strength in scalability, the autonomy, certainly it does things faster than humans.

102
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:18,800
And the humans currently are good at identifying what problems are important, at least in the scientific field I'm at, biological science or medicine, still humans know which problems are important.

103
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:35,180
So I think that's currently where we are, but if we see the longer term future, I think education is a really important point we really need to think about.

104
00:12:35,406 --> 00:12:46,720
You know, how we should prepare the next generation of scientists and society more broadly for a world that's increasingly shaped by AI.

105
00:12:47,246 --> 00:12:56,840
So I think, you know, one of the skills that's really important, this is applied to scientists and then non-scientists, you know, how AI works.

106
00:12:57,880 --> 00:13:03,700
You know, the modern AI is basically, it learns statistical patterns from the data.

107
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:09,520
So humans should know how it works, what it can do, what it cannot do, and then how it can fail.

108
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:16,720
So other than, you know, scientist skills, you know, to identify important problems and so on.

109
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:20,900
but for younger generation, I think important skill is, you know.

110
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:31,700
So this AI literacy along with solid statistical foundation, I think it should be a fundamental part of education.

111
00:13:33,166 --> 00:13:36,560
You know, a lot like, you know, learning math and then our languages.

112
00:13:37,220 --> 00:13:37,620
Thank you.

113
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:38,212
Ron, briefly.

114
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:38,560
Yeah.

115
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:49,200
At the very highest level, you need to be positioned to make sure that AI is your slave and you're accountable for what it does in whatever job.

116
00:13:49,582 --> 00:13:53,720
If you're going to be a plumber, then you need to make sure you're in charge of what happens.

117
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:55,620
That's probably pretty safe, plumbing.

118
00:13:56,440 --> 00:14:00,280
If you are a computer scientist, then you've got to make sure you're in charge of things.

119
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:09,340
So I think at the very highest level, making sure you're in charge of AI and it doesn't just do stuff for you is the skill that people need to learn.

120
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:11,300
I

121
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:29,040
think, historically speaking, de-skilling always happens in our science research history because, for instance, in the 19th century, every scientist has to build their own machine in order to make an experiment, but we don't do that anymore.

122
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:47,320
So de-skilling itself is not a big problem, but the problem is, as you mentioned, we have to focus what kind of skills we have to maintain and train continuously, even when those skills can be automated by AI.

123
00:14:47,630 --> 00:14:53,380
Because some of the skills are related to the responsibility, accountability, and ethical judgment.

124
00:14:53,580 --> 00:15:01,572
And we need to focus on this kind of skill to keep on working as a real competent scientist.

125
00:15:01,998 --> 00:15:03,180
Thank you very much indeed.

126
00:15:03,278 --> 00:15:04,300
Thank you to all of you.

127
00:15:05,230 --> 00:15:09,220
You'll meet all three of these people later during the day in different conversations.

128
00:15:09,870 --> 00:15:14,800
It's been fascinating to listen to you and you've really highlighted some of the areas that the program will be touching on during the day.

129
00:15:15,246 --> 00:15:18,120
It was a perfect demonstration of what the conversations we're trying to get to.

130
00:15:18,382 --> 00:15:20,360
So thank you all very much indeed.

131
00:15:28,630 --> 00:15:29,252
Thank you.

132
00:15:29,790 --> 00:15:42,010
So next we move on to the main part of the morning program, which is to ask the question of how AI might change the nature of discovery and to introduce that section of the programme.

133
00:15:42,702 --> 00:15:44,410
We have a short talk by Alison Noble.

134
00:15:44,530 --> 00:16:01,010
Alison is Professor of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Oxford, and she also chaired the Royal Society of the UK's working group on science in the age of AI, which produced a report in 2024 and is going to produce another report in 2027.

135
00:16:01,150 --> 00:16:05,924
I believe, and she's going to summarise briefly the findings of those reports and where it's taking us.

136
00:16:06,090 --> 00:16:07,250
Please welcome Alison Noble.

137
00:16:14,580 --> 00:16:29,700
I'm delighted to be here to talk to you a little bit about the Royal Society's work and some of its findings and it was focused particularly on how the scientific process is evolving and what a scientist does and how that's changing across science.

138
00:16:30,574 --> 00:16:44,300
The first report was published in 2024, it followed many discussions with scientists getting them together in round tables and particularly the question was, what do scientists say mattered at that time?

139
00:16:45,102 --> 00:16:56,324
The big concerns were about data access, requiring the requirements for large volumes and quantities of data, and the importance of curation of well-representative data.

140
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:02,860
And of course, that's an international question for many of us, is how can we work together with data?

141
00:17:04,240 --> 00:17:06,560
Computational infrastructure is also very challenging.

142
00:17:07,319 --> 00:17:21,640
Traditionally, science being based on the desk, with your computer on your desk, moving to now require regional and national investment in infrastructures so that you can have the compute capacity to work on AI.

143
00:17:23,246 --> 00:17:26,359
The roles of interdisciplinarity are also changing.

144
00:17:26,766 --> 00:17:38,040
That means how science disciplines work together, computer scientists with clinical medicine, as in my case, but also going across discipline barriers.

145
00:17:38,540 --> 00:17:44,964
And that means you're going from siloed areas, starting to work in teams, so the importance of team science.

146
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:50,380
And in science, this creates big challenges where competition drives a lot of what we do.

147
00:17:50,766 --> 00:17:56,708
And the notion that partnership might drive some of the future, how do we set up reward systems to manage that?

148
00:17:58,340 --> 00:18:01,620
Reproducibility has always been a great foundation of science.

149
00:18:01,620 --> 00:18:04,900
you need to be able to reproduce your results to others.

150
00:18:05,390 --> 00:18:07,300
This is challenging when we have black boxes.

151
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:19,364
And this falls under what we call responsible AI, that you need to, if you produce a result, be able to reproduce it, manage data bias, explain the explainability that must come with results.

152
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:25,764
And also be able to offer your models and data in an open way, so openness and transparency.

153
00:18:26,360 --> 00:18:29,860
These are, again, with AI, are much bigger challenges than before.

154
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:36,500
And this is really important so that we generate evidence and knowledge that people can trust.

155
00:18:38,062 --> 00:18:42,700
And on algorithms, the conclusion was they will just evolve, and we've seen that.

156
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:51,580
We've seen convolutional neural networks, transformers, large language models, and more recently, agentic AI and world models come along.

157
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:54,200
And scientists are absorbing all these new technologies.

158
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:59,580
But we must remember that we use the word AI but it's not one thing.

159
00:19:00,100 --> 00:19:05,796
We can have AI as a superpower for discovery with its ability to do pattern recognition predictions.

160
00:19:06,542 --> 00:19:10,160
We can have AI as your assistant to empower an individual.

161
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:15,320
So your research assistant supporting your research assistants is a new concept.

162
00:19:15,940 --> 00:19:21,240
The AI do the coding for you, allows you to do more reflection and thought yourself.

163
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:31,920
and the diverse applications we're seeing across the whole of science from biology, medicine to computer vision, materials, chemistry and things like climate modeling.

164
00:19:32,846 --> 00:19:40,520
So moving on to 2026, we're doing an update of our report and I just thought I'd mention some early findings.

165
00:19:41,518 --> 00:19:45,080
First of all, applications have got deeper rather than broader.

166
00:19:45,966 --> 00:19:57,620
Some of the different disciplines are having different experiences, for example, mathematicians, for example, as we've heard earlier, they're concluding that AI is really creative but challenging who they are as a discipline.

167
00:19:58,260 --> 00:20:04,740
Other areas such as climate scientists have used the AI and maybe understanding some of its limitations as well.

168
00:20:05,262 --> 00:20:10,404
So we're seeing this as the technology diffuses in, we're seeing different experiences.

169
00:20:11,726 --> 00:20:16,920
We're also got new things like AI-based sciences full of things that work before we know why.

170
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:19,880
So do we publish these and figure out why later?

171
00:20:20,238 --> 00:20:25,796
So there's a new type of science publication and we need to think carefully how we manage that.

172
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:30,000
Reproducibility though remains one of our challenges.

173
00:20:31,726 --> 00:20:43,880
And also using large language models, they're built around sequences and languages and the outputs do not always reflect reality or the scientific questions we're interested in.

174
00:20:44,206 --> 00:20:46,420
so we need to be careful how we manage that.

175
00:20:47,502 --> 00:20:58,948
And this brings on to the human factors and we need to develop deeper understanding in science of how we trust results, things like cognitive overloading and over-reliance.

176
00:20:59,534 --> 00:21:04,580
We need to particularly think about judgment and advice-taking in the context of science.

177
00:21:05,220 --> 00:21:16,676
For example, it's fine to ask AI to query your results and the evidence to confirm a hypothesis, but you mustn't become over-reliant on it.

178
00:21:17,486 --> 00:21:22,720
And finally, we have what we call the apprentice problem, and that's a shared anxiety.

179
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:35,716
There is a lot of uncertainty about career paths of future experts if team roles are changing and AI is doing tasks that scientists used to spend time doing in the past.

180
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:37,900
So, AI is a tool;

181
00:21:38,030 --> 00:21:49,600
it's an incredibly powerful tool, but we need to ensure science, and scientists do not become less creative and are not less able to tackle the problems that do not follow trends.

182
00:21:49,902 --> 00:21:52,940
and we mustn't follow trends.

183
00:21:53,198 --> 00:22:06,596
and the dominant approaches too much, and we need to create, continue to create the opportunities for the unexpected scientific discoveries which have for centuries been the foundation of scientific advancement.

184
00:22:07,406 --> 00:22:07,840
Thank you.

185
00:22:18,926 --> 00:22:20,030
Thank you very much indeed.

186
00:22:21,006 --> 00:22:30,770
So now we move from Alison's talk into our first panel on this subject, which is going to ask the question of what has AI contributed to science so far.

187
00:22:31,410 --> 00:22:35,050
For this we have three wonderful panelists and a great moderator.

188
00:22:35,310 --> 00:22:44,110
We have Min Kyung Baek from SNU, SNU, and she worked with Nobel Laureate David Baker at the University of Washington on development of Rosetta Fold.

189
00:22:45,038 --> 00:22:52,470
We have Honglak Lee, who is Chief AI Scientist at LG AI Research and also Associate Professor at the University of Michigan.

190
00:22:53,230 --> 00:23:06,372
David McMillan, 2021 Nobel Laureate in Chemistry for the development of organocatalysis, and the conversation will be moderated by Seonju Kim, who is a professor in the Department of Computer Science at Yonsei University.

191
00:23:06,894 --> 00:23:08,370
So, please welcome them all

192
00:23:14,770 --> 00:23:15,990
in a minute.

193
00:23:19,726 --> 00:23:20,004
Thank you.

194
00:23:24,430 --> 00:23:27,268
So, Minkyung Honglak.

195
00:23:34,862 --> 00:23:36,580
Okay, so let's just start right away.

196
00:23:36,686 --> 00:23:37,900
So, I'll start with Minkyung.

197
00:23:38,080 --> 00:23:43,440
So, you've been obviously heavily involved in the development of Rosetta Fold.

198
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:51,492
So, let me ask you, after Alpha Fold and Rosetta Fold, how much has Bio has changed?

199
00:23:51,740 --> 00:23:55,240
What is the current status of Bio and AI?

200
00:23:55,240 --> 00:24:03,332
Well, I would say still biologists have the same question, how biological phenomena actually happens.

201
00:24:04,060 --> 00:24:16,484
So, as a, I mean, biologist, but trained in chemistry major, I always have a question like how biological phenomena is handled or governed by molecular interactions.

202
00:24:17,582 --> 00:24:21,636
So to me, AlphaFold and RosettaFold is just a starting point.

203
00:24:22,318 --> 00:24:40,356
So, Alpha4 can predict the protein structures with reasonable accuracy, so that now the structure biologists, I mean, in long times the protein structure itself was a major bottleneck to study proteins function, but now AI has removed that bottlenecks.

204
00:24:41,140 --> 00:24:55,400
And now people can focus on their original question, like how proteins actually do their work by understanding their molecular mechanisms and understanding their interactions and how the effect of mutation can cause some disease.

205
00:24:56,398 --> 00:25:00,520
So I would say the just question has been changed.

206
00:25:01,358 --> 00:25:04,980
So basically, AI just removed one of the bottlenecks.

207
00:25:05,160 --> 00:25:22,320
And we tried to take another, the next bottleneck, like how we can predict interactions or how we can predict dynamics of the proteins and how we can predict the changes of function depending on molecular, I mean the changes in molecular interaction in certain condition.

208
00:25:23,060 --> 00:25:32,900
So now the AI tries, I mean we actually use those AI tools to try to solve that kind of question, our next bottlenecks.

209
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:46,580
So as a AI developers as well, I'm currently focusing on developing AI models to understand interactions of the proteins as well as designing proteins to control those kind of interactions.

210
00:25:47,380 --> 00:25:52,880
So that's how AI actually contributed the biology field.

211
00:25:53,460 --> 00:25:56,000
Okay, so just let me ask you one question.

212
00:25:56,160 --> 00:26:03,400
So if you're building an AI model for a bio, like how much capability do you need from AI and bio?

213
00:26:03,740 --> 00:26:05,520
Like if you're recruiting a student?

214
00:26:06,350 --> 00:26:17,300
I always prefer the biology or chemistry major, which have some on domain knowledge because, I mean, it's kind of irony.

215
00:26:17,520 --> 00:26:24,860
When AI becomes better and better, then you don't need any AI specialties because AI can code.

216
00:26:25,240 --> 00:26:28,400
AI can write all the programming code.

217
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:32,440
And also AI can also suggest some AI architectures to solve the problem.

218
00:26:32,878 --> 00:26:41,960
But it's really important to understand what is the real question or what is the real problem to solve.

219
00:26:42,126 --> 00:26:47,556
So that requires domain knowledge, that's why I usually focus on, yeah, those fields.

220
00:26:47,918 --> 00:26:48,780
Okay, thank you.

221
00:26:49,230 --> 00:26:50,540
So I'll go to Hongrak.

222
00:26:51,118 --> 00:27:03,380
So I think Hongrak brings really interesting and unique viewpoint because Hongrak himself is a very distinguished AI researcher, but now he's leading one of the biggest AI research labs in Korea.

223
00:27:04,100 --> 00:27:09,284
So Hongrak, you're actually one of the researchers who started deep learning research.

224
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:17,604
So were you actually surprised how fast this has happened and can you tell me about your work at LG?

225
00:27:18,240 --> 00:27:35,260
Yes, so it has been very surprising because probably before the generative model, we were developing individual models, like it's basically called deep learning.

226
00:27:35,406 --> 00:27:50,140
We build neural network, train the data, and then we show some effectiveness of this model for these individual problems, and essentially we were conquering one after another, and it was very exciting.

227
00:27:50,254 --> 00:27:56,620
But then after this so-called Chat GPT, like generated models came out.

228
00:27:56,620 --> 00:28:10,200
these models are much smarter and actually can do a lot of general purpose tasks, such as reading papers and maybe.

229
00:28:10,200 --> 00:28:32,900
Understanding, and nowadays, the model can also reason, so synthesizing from multiple source of information, like maybe looking at some papers from chemistry, and then look at the diagram, like pictures of how these molecules look like, and then translate that into some molecular, like representation.

230
00:28:32,900 --> 00:28:36,680
and basically you just combine all the relevant information.

231
00:28:36,686 --> 00:28:55,660
And then somehow this kind of ability of AI can synthesize some new hypothesis, and in particular in LGA research, we have been developing so-called molecular property foundation model;

232
00:28:56,334 --> 00:28:57,720
we call it XR discovery.

233
00:28:58,600 --> 00:29:12,880
And the basic idea is that we basically gather all the available information like papers, patents, and all kinds of multi-modal information like a diagram of molecules and how they interact and so on.

234
00:29:12,940 --> 00:29:19,684
And then we try to build a foundation model that can predict the molecular property.

235
00:29:20,340 --> 00:29:34,060
And also So not only predicting the property, we also want to have some model that can generate some possible feasible pathway to synthesize them.

236
00:29:34,446 --> 00:29:45,572
So then we predict various other properties, like retrosynthesis, and also predicting some side effects of these synthesis, and so on.

237
00:29:47,342 --> 00:29:53,860
So yeah, we were very surprised that these models Those can actually be used for various purposes.

238
00:29:55,460 --> 00:30:11,660
So concretely, we had some successful projects on developing some new materials for batteries, like a cathode and electrolytes, some kind of property, optimizing for these properties.

239
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:31,840
But also recently, we have been collaborating with LG Household and Health Care for developing some materials that can help this cosmetic or skin products to reach through the skin.

240
00:30:32,020 --> 00:30:40,516
So basically that improves the skin permeability and this can basically increase the effectiveness of these products.

241
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:48,640
But also another very interesting thing we found is that very similar molecule can be useful for hair loss.

242
00:30:49,902 --> 00:31:06,580
Probably one of the very practical problem many people want to solve and yeah we actually are also collaborating with LG Household and Healthcare for turning this into actually a product hopefully sometime soon.

243
00:31:07,260 --> 00:31:16,932
So I see that there is a lot of serendipity basically you develop something interest but then discover that this can be also useful for other purposes.

244
00:31:17,380 --> 00:31:25,200
So I think this is a very powerful case study of AI.

245
00:31:26,126 --> 00:31:43,748
So to summarize, what we do with this excellent discovery foundation model is basically help screening these candidate molecules from a vast space of search for this molecular space.

246
00:31:43,790 --> 00:31:49,740
and then this can be very helpful for generating some candidates.

247
00:31:49,740 --> 00:31:56,708
and then we verify them with the actual web lab and some other physical experiments.

248
00:31:57,160 --> 00:32:14,580
So I also maybe would like to touch upon some projects that we are working on some biology area like developing some new antibody for cancer treatment but probably I will move on for the sake of time yeah.

249
00:32:15,054 --> 00:32:17,284
Okay so we may actually come back to that later.

250
00:32:17,940 --> 00:32:27,332
So David, so how much is AI impacting chemistry and kind of added to that how much AI are you using to do your research?

251
00:32:27,790 --> 00:32:29,880
So those are two very big questions.

252
00:32:30,200 --> 00:32:36,640
Before I begin though I want to start off by saying thank you for inviting me here to Korea to Seoul.

253
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:43,620
My wife is Korean and so it's fantastic to get the opportunity to come back to Seoul.

254
00:32:43,700 --> 00:32:44,440
I love to come here.

255
00:32:44,960 --> 00:32:55,420
I've been to COEX many times, almost always to go shopping, not to be in an auditorium talking about AI, so this is a very new thing for me.

256
00:32:56,440 --> 00:33:00,860
But for chemistry, chemistry is an enormous field, it's an enormous area.

257
00:33:00,980 --> 00:33:06,720
There's so many different ways in which AI is already impacting chemistry.

258
00:33:07,310 --> 00:33:24,460
People will talk about materials, people will talk about pharmaceuticals and medicines, many different things, but at the moment most of that work is optimization, which I would say is low-hanging fruit, which makes it sound not important.

259
00:33:24,760 --> 00:33:28,820
It turns out it's incredibly important to be able to optimize and accelerate.

260
00:33:29,582 --> 00:33:38,300
If you think about medicines, you think about materials, you think about all these different ways that we can impact our world by accelerating things, AI is really incredible.

261
00:33:39,400 --> 00:33:51,880
But I think for chemistry, what we're now seeing, and a number of people actually talked about this in the opening speeches, it's not about just taking the things that we know and do already and making them much faster.

262
00:33:52,590 --> 00:33:58,020
It's about creating completely new questions that, as human beings, we're not even thinking about right now.

263
00:33:58,100 --> 00:34:01,300
And that, to me, is the most exciting part about AI for chemistry.

264
00:34:02,222 --> 00:34:13,380
And, for example, I'll give you a couple of examples, but if you think about climate change, how do you use AI to think about climate change and things like that?

265
00:34:13,480 --> 00:34:16,460
And you're already beginning to see some really clever ideas.

266
00:34:17,262 --> 00:34:26,480
For example, one of my favorite is when you look in the sky and you see an airplane going past in the sky and you see this white line coming behind it called contrails.

267
00:34:27,181 --> 00:34:32,600
These contrails actually contribute more to global warming than actually the CO2 that comes from airplanes.

268
00:34:32,880 --> 00:34:34,659
And people don't really think about that a lot.

269
00:34:35,406 --> 00:34:47,840
So one of the things you're now seeing is really smart scientists thinking about ways to change the molecules which are being used in fuels so that you can then use those and not create those problems.

270
00:34:48,525 --> 00:34:51,159
And then that brings you back to the question, well how do you do that?

271
00:34:51,239 --> 00:34:54,280
Then AI can help you to think about how do you design those molecules?

272
00:34:54,926 --> 00:34:57,540
What are the things which were abundant on earth that you can start to use?

273
00:34:57,582 --> 00:34:59,560
That's just one way of thinking about doing it.

274
00:35:00,782 --> 00:35:10,700
The second thing I'll say, and this goes back to the question of biology and medicine, is you see so many different people right now talking about AI is creating medicines.

275
00:35:11,630 --> 00:35:15,000
I think that's somewhat true, but it's not completely true.

276
00:35:15,900 --> 00:35:23,980
You see what's really happening is you're seeing human beings that are being amplified in their discovery of medicines by AI.

277
00:35:24,180 --> 00:35:32,260
But at the moment, AI cannot de novo predict a molecule that's going to be a medicine, even though people tend to sort of talk about it in those ways.

278
00:35:32,846 --> 00:35:38,480
But as time marches forward, that is becoming more and more, the capabilities are increasing.

279
00:35:39,214 --> 00:35:50,700
But one of the things which is going to be really problematic, I think, or difficult, is the complexity of biology, which means you have to gain new insights and new learnings and more serendipity along the way to be able to sort of do that.

280
00:35:51,374 --> 00:35:57,620
So from my perspective, you know, this is just the beginning for AI and how it impacts chemistry.

281
00:35:57,700 --> 00:36:10,692
There are so many different things to do, but it's ultimately gonna come back to the questions and the questions a lot of the times have to come from the creativity of human beings to think about how we're actually gonna use AI in completely different ways than we're using it right now.

282
00:36:11,950 --> 00:36:13,880
And about the question, how much AI?

283
00:36:14,060 --> 00:36:14,720
How much do we use?

284
